1. The 4-Layer Assertion Strategy
    When you receive a response object (usually via the requests or httpx library), you should evaluate it from the outside in. If an outer layer fails, the test should fail immediately before evaluating the inner layers.

        Network & Status (The Envelope): Did the request succeed at the HTTP level?
        Headers & Metadata (The Routing): Is the server returning the data type and caching rules we expect?
        Schema Validation (The Contract): Does the structure of the JSON match our expected data types? (This is what separates beginners from professionals).
        Business Logic (The Content): Are the specific values correct based on our test setup?


## pydantic ###
Pydantic is the standard tool in Python for data validation, enforcing type hints at runtime. 
To validate request and response data, you define schemas by inheriting from Pydantic's BaseModel.

Generating Pydantic models from an OpenAPI or JSON Schema 
    pip install datamodel-code-generator
    -> downlaod schema.yaml from http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/schema/
    Use: 
        datamodel-codegen --input schema.yaml --input-file-type openapi --output pydantic_models.py --output-model-type pydantic_v2.BaseModel --target-python-version 3.11 --use-annotated --use-standard-collections --strict-nullable --formatters black isort --openapi-scopes schemas paths


## Naming conventions ##
1. Test Organization: Grouping OK and Not-OK Tests
    - Keep both success (OK) and failure (Not-OK) tests in the same class.
    - A test class should act as a container for a single endpoint or a specific resource. This makes your test suite highly cohesive. If someone breaks the /api/user_management/token/ endpoint, all failing tests will be localized to TestTokenObtain.

    Pro-Tip: Use @pytest.mark.parametrize for your negative tests. Instead of writing five different test methods for bad passwords, missing usernames, and invalid emails, write one test_token_obtain_failure method and parametrize the payload and expected status codes (e.g., 400, 401).

2. Naming Conventions for Pytest
    - Good naming conventions tell a developer exactly what broke without them having to read the test body.
    - Files: Name them after the domain or resource (e.g., test_auth.py or test_user_management.py).
    - Classes: Name them Test<Action><Resource> (e.g., TestTokenObtain or TestCreateToken).
    - Methods: Follow the test_<action>_<scenario>_<expected_result> pattern.
    - Good: test_obtain_token_valid_credentials_returns_200
    - Good: test_obtain_token_invalid_password_returns_401


### AI approach from postman ###
3. The LLM Approach (ChatGPT / Claude / Copilot)
If you have a complex Postman workflow that includes a lot of custom Javascript in the "Pre-request Scripts" or "Tests" tabs, standard converters will usually fail. They only convert the requests, not your custom logic.

For complex workflows, the most effective modern workflow is to feed the Postman JSON directly into an LLM.

How to do it:

Export the specific folder from your Postman collection as JSON.

Paste it into your AI tool with a prompt like this:

"Here is a Postman collection JSON. Please convert this workflow into a Python Pytest file using the requests library. Convert the Postman variables into a Pytest fixture that yields an API client, and translate the Postman Javascript tests into Python assert statements."

What to watch out for (The Limitations)
If you use automated tools, you will almost always have to clean up the code slightly. Postman uses a lot of double-curly-brace variables (like {{base_url}} or {{auth_token}}).

When tools convert this, they often leave those variables as strings in Python (e.g., requests.get("{{base_url}}/users")). You will need to manually hook those strings up to your conftest.py fixtures or environment variables so they actually resolve during the test.

## Api clients instances #####
Dedicated Client Instances: Use an unauthenticated ApiClient for public endpoints (login, registration, account activation) and create a separate AuthenticatedApiClient instance specifically for user/admin operations.
